# xrlenv operator environment — copy to .env and fill values in.
#
# Anything in this file is read into os.environ on `import xrlenv`,
# but only if the variable isn't already exported in your shell —
# shell-exported values always win. Disable the auto-load entirely
# with `export XRLENV_DOTENV=off` if you prefer pure-shell config.
#
# .env is gitignored. Don't commit your real values.

######################## Fast CheatSheet #########################
# XRLENV_NODE_TOKEN=
# XRLENV_CONSUMER_TOKEN=
# XRLENV_OPERATOR_TOKEN=
# XRLENV_GRPC_HOST=
# XRLENV_GRPC_PORT=
# XRLENV_REGISTRY_STORAGE=
# XRLENV_MIRROR_REGISTRY_HOST=
# XRLENV_MIRROR_REGISTRY_PORT=
# XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_STORAGE=
# XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_HOST=
# XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_PORT=
# XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_HTTP_SECRET=

# DOCKERHUB_USER=
# DOCKERHUB_TOKEN=

# XRLENV_BENCHMARK_CACHE=
# XRLENV_HOME=~/.xrlenv

######################## Fast CheatSheet END #########################

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required for consumer-side workloads (SDK, drop-in, smokes/examples)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# place to store the xrlenv credentials and state
XRLENV_HOME=~/.xrlenv

# Where your control plane gRPC server is reachable from the host
# running this consumer (your laptop, login node, GPU node, etc.).
# Use 127.0.0.1 when control plane is on the same host or you have
# an SSH/VS-Code port-forward terminating on loopback.
XRLENV_GRPC_HOST=<your control plane IP>
XRLENV_GRPC_PORT=50051

# Bearer token for consumer-tier RPCs (Client.grpc, drop-in,
# from_env). Required when the control plane has any tokens issued.
# Get it via `cat ~/.xrlenv/secrets/consumer.token` on the control
# plane after `xrlenv tokens issue consumer`.
XRLENV_CONSUMER_TOKEN=<your consumer token>
XRLENV_OPERATOR_TOKEN=<your operator token>
XRLENV_NODE_TOKEN=<your node token>
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Benchmark / harness integrations
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# terminal-bench-2 / harbor task cache. Run
# `bash examples/benchmarks-onboarding/terminal-bench-2/scripts/populate-harbor-cache.sh`
# to populate. The default below points at the conventional location;
# override if your cluster mounts a shared cache elsewhere.
XRLENV_BENCHMARK_CACHE=<your_path>/xrlenv_benchmark_cache

# Docker Hub Personal Access Token for the build-plan generators
# (terminal-bench-2, swebench-verified). Probes against Docker Hub's
# manifest API are rate-limited at ~100 / 6h per source IP when
# unauthenticated — a 500-entry SWE-bench Verified `--all` sweep
# blows through that budget around entry ~100 and silently falls
# back to a conservative size_hint that makes FFD reject the plan.
# Set these to your Docker Hub username + PAT (Business/Team/Pro
# accounts get a much higher cap, often unlimited). The pull-through
# registry mirror (below) reuses these for its upstream Docker Hub auth.
# See docs/technical_details/images/build_plan.md § "Docker Hub probing".
DOCKERHUB_USER=
DOCKERHUB_TOKEN=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pull-through registry mirror (Phase A) — only on the host that runs
# deploy/registry/run-registry-proxy.sh (the control-plane / registry box)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A registry:3 pull-through proxy in front of Docker Hub, backed by FSx, so
# worker image re-pulls go over the internal network instead of Docker Hub.
# It authenticates upstream with DOCKERHUB_USER / DOCKERHUB_TOKEN (above).
# Cache retention is config-driven (proxy.ttl in deploy/registry/config.yml).
# See deploy/registry/README.md. Both keys below are optional (defaults shown).

# FSx blob-store path for the mirror cache. Must be a shared (Lustre/NFS)
# mount, NOT the dockerd data-root (overlay can't live on Lustre). Default:
# /path/to/data$USER/xrlenv-registry/proxy
XRLENV_REGISTRY_STORAGE=<your registry storage path>

# Host port the mirror publishes (workers point registry-mirrors here).
XRLENV_MIRROR_REGISTRY_HOST=<your mirror registry host>
XRLENV_MIRROR_REGISTRY_PORT=<your mirror registry port>

# Host + port of the PRIVATE writable registry (`:5011`) — required only for benchmarks that
# BUILD images and push them (seta = type:git; terminalworld + lhtb's 6 rebuild tasks =
# type:local). The build/push scripts + those run_full_sweep.sh address it as
# `${XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_HOST}:${XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_PORT}` (e.g. seta's README push
# command), so BOTH must be set. Prebuilt-image benchmarks (swebench_verified, deep_swe, tb2.1)
# don't need either.
XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_HOST=<your private-registry host>
XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_PORT=5011
XRLENV_PRIVATE_REGISTRY_HTTP_SECRET=private-registry-secret

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LLM API keys (only for in-container agent examples)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Required for `agent_inside_container.py` and any installed-agent
# workflow that drives a real LLM. Pick ONE provider key — claude-code,
# aider, codex, etc. each prefer their default vendor.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Operator-side (rarely needed in consumer .env)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# These are typically set by `xrlenv up` / `xrlenv-node serve` from
# the operator's deploy config, not from a consumer's .env. Listed
# here for completeness so an all-in-one-node operator can use the
# same .env across roles.

# Root for ALL control-plane operator state: state.db, secrets/, runs/,
# the admin trajectory cache, and the node-side build-context cache all
# live under it. Default: ~/.xrlenv. Set XRLENV_HOME to relocate the whole
# tree as a group. The use case is running TWO clusters (e.g. a dev cluster
# beside prod) on hosts that share one operator $HOME over FSx/NFS: give the
# dev checkout's .env its own XRLENV_HOME so its state.db and token store
# never collide with prod's. (A second `xrlenv up` left on the default path
# would open prod's SQLite over a network FS — two writers, corruption — and
# share prod's token store, so a dev token would authenticate against prod.)
# Prefer this over overriding $HOME: $HOME would also relocate ~/.gitconfig,
# ~/.ssh, and ~/.docker/config.json (registry auth); XRLENV_HOME scopes the
# move to xrlenv's own state. Read at `import xrlenv`, so set it in the shell
# or this .env BEFORE launching `xrlenv up` (not mid-process). Leave unset for
# the normal single-cluster setup — fully backward-compatible. `--state-db` /
# `--runs-root` still override those two paths individually; XRLENV_HOME is the
# one switch that also moves secrets/ (which has no `xrlenv up` flag).
# XRLENV_HOME=/path/to/data$USER/Github/xrlenv/.xrlenv

# Used by `xrlenv-node serve` to dial the control plane on startup.
# Distinct from XRLENV_GRPC_HOST: that's "where consumers dial"; this
# is "where the node-agent dials its control plane."
# XRLENV_CONTROL_PLANE=127.0.0.1:50051
# XRLENV_NODE_ID=
# XRLENV_NODE_TOKEN=

# Bearer for operator-tier writes (admin panel POSTs, `xrlenv build
# apply --connect-host`). Typically passed via flag, not env.
# XRLENV_OPERATOR_TOKEN=

# Bearer for read-only admin panel access (issued by `xrlenv tokens
# issue viewer`). The browser flow uses basic auth with this value.
# XRLENV_VIEWER_TOKEN=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Knobs (almost never needed)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# TLS for the gRPC channel. Default: insecure (loopback / private
# network). Set to "true" / "1" / "yes" when control plane terminates
# TLS itself or behind a TLS-terminating proxy.
# XRLENV_GRPC_SECURE=false

# Set to "off" / "false" / "0" / "no" / "disabled" to skip the
# `.env` auto-load entirely. Useful when running tests that want
# strict env isolation from any local .env.
# XRLENV_DOTENV=off

# Per-node raw-container concurrency caps (read by `xrlenv-node serve`).
# Bound how many docker create / destroy / archive ops hit one node's daemon
# at once. Defaults (below) are the measured sweet spots; `0` disables a cap.
# XRLENV_RAW_CREATE_CONCURRENCY=4
# XRLENV_RAW_DESTROY_CONCURRENCY=4
# XRLENV_RAW_ARCHIVE_CONCURRENCY=4
# Tighter, SEPARATE create cap for sysbox (non-runc) containers — sysbox-fs
# pre-register is far slower than a plain runc create, so serialising sysbox
# creates (default 1) stops the transient `pre-register with sysbox-fs …
# DeadlineExceeded` at the source. `0` falls back to XRLENV_RAW_CREATE_CONCURRENCY.
# XRLENV_RAW_SYSBOX_CREATE_CONCURRENCY=1

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sysbox node pool — OPERATOR build-tooling only
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the vendored (source-built) Sysbox binaries live. Read ONLY by the
# build/install/deploy scripts under xrlenv_plugins/sysbox/ (via pin.env) —
# it is NOT a runtime / control-plane / node-agent setting, and CONSUMERS
# never touch it (they select the runtime by name:
# `acquire_container(container_runtime="sysbox-runc")`). Has a default; set
# this only if your vendored binaries live somewhere other than the shared
# /shared-fs path below. See xrlenv_plugins/sysbox/README.md.
# XRLENV_SYSBOX_VENDOR_DIR=/path/to/sysbox-vendor
